</aside><button class="sidebar-toggle" aria-label="Toggle sidebar">☰</button><main class="main"><div class="container"><section class="hero"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Associate</a><span class="sep">›</span><a href="#">Module 3</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>GroupBy &amp; Aggregation</span></div><span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 3 · Lesson 6</span><h1>GroupBy and Aggregation</h1><p class="hero-sub">Master the split-apply-combine pattern with groupby(). Learn to compute group-level statistics, use multiple aggregation functions, apply transformations, and create pivot tables. These techniques are essential for summarizing data and discovering patterns across groups.</p></section><div class="objectives"><h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Understand the split-apply-combine pattern and groupby mechanics</li><li>Apply single and multiple aggregation functions</li><li>Use transform() to broadcast results back to original shape</li><li>Create and reshape data with pivot tables</li><li>Generate cross-tabulations for categorical data analysis</li><li>Optimize groupby operations for performance</li></ul></div><section class="section" id="groupby-basics"><span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span><h2>The GroupBy Pattern</h2><p>GroupBy implements the split-apply-combine pattern: split your data into groups, apply a function to each group, and combine the results. This is how most data analysis flows—you rarely want statistics on the entire dataset; you want them within meaningful categories.</p><p>Think of groupby like sorting items into bins, processing each bin separately, then combining the results. It's one of the most powerful operations in data analysis.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">pandas</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">pd</span>

<span class="cm"># Create sales dataset</span>
<span class="va">sales</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'salesperson'</span>: [<span class="st">'Alice'</span>, <span class="st">'Bob'</span>, <span class="st">'Alice'</span>, <span class="st">'Charlie'</span>, <span class="st">'Bob'</span>, <span class="st">'Charlie'</span>],
    <span class="st">'region'</span>: [<span class="st">'North'</span>, <span class="st">'South'</span>, <span class="st">'North'</span>, <span class="st">'East'</span>, <span class="st">'South'</span>, <span class="st">'East'</span>],
    <span class="st">'amount'</span>: [<span class="nm">5000</span>, <span class="nm">3000</span>, <span class="nm">7000</span>, <span class="nm">4000</span>, <span class="nm">6000</span>, <span class="nm">5500</span>]
})

<span class="cm"># Split by salesperson, calculate total sales</span>
<span class="va">by_person</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'salesperson'</span>)[<span class="st">'amount'</span>].<span class="fn">sum</span>()
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">by_person</span>)
<span class="cm"># Alice    12000</span>
<span class="cm"># Bob       9000</span>
<span class="cm"># Charlie   9500</span>

<span class="cm"># Group by multiple columns</span>
<span class="va">by_person_region</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>([<span class="st">'salesperson'</span>, <span class="st">'region'</span>])[<span class="st">'amount'</span>].<span class="fn">sum</span>()</code></pre><div class="key-concept"><strong>Key Concept:</strong> groupby returns a GroupBy object, which is iterable. The chain groupby().column.aggregate() extracts a specific column and applies operations. Use groupby() without column selection to operate on multiple columns.</div><div class="pipeline">
<div class="pipeline-stage">
<div class="pipeline-title">Split</div>
<div class="pipeline-content">Divide by group key (salesperson)</div>
</div>
<div class="pipeline-stage">
<div class="pipeline-title">Apply</div>
<div class="pipeline-content">Compute sum() for each group</div>
</div>
<div class="pipeline-stage">
<div class="pipeline-title">Combine</div>
<div class="pipeline-content">Return Series with group labels</div>
</div>
</div></section><section class="section" id="aggregation"><span class="section-label">Aggregation</span><h2>Multiple Aggregation Functions</h2><p>The agg() method (aggregation) is your workhorse for applying multiple functions to grouped data. You can apply different functions to different columns, creating rich summary DataFrames.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Single aggregation function on multiple columns</span>
<span class="va">summary1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'region'</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>({
    <span class="st">'amount'</span>: <span class="st">'sum'</span>,
    <span class="st">'salesperson'</span>: <span class="st">'count'</span>
})

<span class="cm"># Multiple functions on same column - use list notation</span>
<span class="va">summary2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'region'</span>)[<span class="st">'amount'</span>].<span class="fn">agg</span>([<span class="st">'sum'</span>, <span class="st">'mean'</span>, <span class="st">'std'</span>, <span class="st">'min'</span>, <span class="st">'max'</span>])

<span class="cm"># Named aggregations for clarity</span>
<span class="va">summary3</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'region'</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>(
    <span class="va">total_sales</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="st">'amount'</span>, <span class="st">'sum'</span>),
    <span class="va">avg_sale</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="st">'amount'</span>, <span class="st">'mean'</span>),
    <span class="va">sale_count</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="st">'amount'</span>, <span class="st">'count'</span>)
)

<span class="cm"># Custom functions</span>
<span class="va">summary4</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'region'</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>({
    <span class="st">'amount'</span>: [<span class="st">'sum'</span>, <span class="st">'mean'</span>, <span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">x</span>: <span class="va">x</span>.<span class="fn">max</span>() <span class="op">-</span> <span class="va">x</span>.<span class="fn">min</span>()]
})  <span class="cm"># Calculates range (max - min)</span></code></pre><div class="compare-grid">
<div class="compare-item">
<div class="compare-title">.sum() / .mean() / .max()</div>
<div class="compare-content">Single aggregation in one line. Returns Series with group labels as index.</div>
</div>
<div class="compare-item">
<div class="compare-title">.agg() / .aggregate()</div>
<div class="compare-content">Apply multiple functions at once. Dictionary maps columns to functions, returning DataFrame.</div>
</div>
</div></section><section class="section" id="transform"><span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span><h2>Transform: Broadcasting Results</h2><p>While agg() reduces data to one row per group, transform() returns a result for every row in the original DataFrame. This is crucial for feature engineering and conditional calculations based on group statistics.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Calculate percentage of group total for each row</span>
<span class="va">sales</span>[<span class="st">'pct_of_region'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> (
    <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'region'</span>)[<span class="st">'amount'</span>]
    .<span class="fn">transform</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">x</span>: <span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="va">x</span>.<span class="fn">sum</span>())
)

<span class="cm"># Normalize each group to have zero mean</span>
<span class="va">sales</span>[<span class="st">'amount_normalized'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> (
    <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'region'</span>)[<span class="st">'amount'</span>]
    .<span class="fn">transform</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">x</span>: <span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">-</span> <span class="va">x</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>())
)

<span class="cm"># Flag top performer in each region</span>
<span class="va">sales</span>[<span class="st">'is_regional_top'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> (
    <span class="va">sales</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'region'</span>)[<span class="st">'amount'</span>]
    .<span class="fn">transform</span>(<span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">x</span>: <span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">==</span> <span class="va">x</span>.<span class="fn">max</span>())
)</code></pre><div class="callout-box tip"><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> transform() maintains the original shape and index, making it perfect for creating new features. It's especially valuable when you need to compare each row against its group's statistics.</div></section><section class="section" id="pivot"><span class="section-label">Data Reshaping</span><h2>Pivot Tables and Cross-Tabulation</h2><p>Pivot tables reshape your data, turning group keys into row/column headers. Cross-tabulation (crosstab) counts occurrences of categorical combinations, revealing frequency patterns.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Create sample transaction data</span>
<span class="va">transactions</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'date'</span>: <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">date_range</span>(<span class="st">'2024-01-01'</span>, <span class="va">periods</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">30</span>),
    <span class="st">'region'</span>: [<span class="st">'North'</span>, <span class="st">'South'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">15</span>,
    <span class="st">'product'</span>: [<span class="st">'A'</span>, <span class="st">'B'</span>, <span class="st">'C'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">10</span>,
    <span class="st">'revenue'</span>: <span class="im">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randint</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">1000</span>, <span class="nm">30</span>)
})

<span class="cm"># Pivot: rows = region, columns = product, values = revenue sum</span>
<span class="va">pivot_table</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">transactions</span>.<span class="fn">pivot_table</span>(
    <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'region'</span>,
    <span class="va">columns</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'product'</span>,
    <span class="va">values</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'revenue'</span>,
    <span class="va">aggfunc</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'sum'</span>,
    <span class="va">fill_value</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Cross-tabulation for categorical counts</span>
<span class="va">crosstab</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">crosstab</span>(
    <span class="va">transactions</span>[<span class="st">'region'</span>],
    <span class="va">transactions</span>[<span class="st">'product'</span>],
    <span class="va">margins</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">True</span>  <span class="cm"># Add row and column totals</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Multiple aggregations in pivot</span>
<span class="va">multi_agg</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">transactions</span>.<span class="fn">pivot_table</span>(
    <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'region'</span>,
    <span class="va">columns</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'product'</span>,
    <span class="va">values</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'revenue'</span>,
    <span class="va">aggfunc</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'sum'</span>, <span class="st">'mean'</span>, <span class="st">'count'</span>]
)</code></pre><div class="term-grid">
<div class="term-item">
<div class="term-name">pivot_table()</div>
<div class="term-desc">Reshapes data with group keys becoming row/column headers. Automatically aggregates values when multiple exist per cell.</div>
</div>
<div class="term-item">
<div class="term-name">crosstab()</div>
<div class="term-desc">Creates frequency tables for categorical variables. Counts occurrences by default, useful for understanding relationships.</div>
</div>
<div class="term-item">
<div class="term-name">aggfunc</span></div>
<div class="term-desc">Aggregation function for pivot tables. Common options: 'sum', 'mean', 'count', 'min', 'max', or custom functions.</div>
</div>
<div class="term-item">
<div class="term-name">margins=True</span></div>
<div class="term-desc">Adds total rows and columns to crosstab, showing grand totals and subtotals.</div>
</div>
</div></section><section class="section" id="practice"><span class="section-label">Hands-On Practice</span><h2>Real-World Data Analysis: E-Commerce Dashboard</h2><p>Let's analyze a realistic e-commerce dataset using all groupby techniques to create an executive dashboard.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="cm"># Create realistic e-commerce dataset</span>
<span class="va">orders</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'order_id'</span>: <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="nm">1000</span>, <span class="nm">1100</span>),
    <span class="st">'date'</span>: <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">date_range</span>(<span class="st">'2024-01-01'</span>, <span class="va">periods</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>),
    <span class="st">'region'</span>: <span class="im">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">choice</span>([<span class="st">'US'</span>, <span class="st">'EU'</span>, <span class="st">'APAC'</span>], <span class="nm">100</span>),
    <span class="st">'category'</span>: <span class="im">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">choice</span>([<span class="st">'Electronics'</span>, <span class="st">'Clothing'</span>, <span class="st">'Books'</span>], <span class="nm">100</span>),
    <span class="st">'amount'</span>: <span class="im">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randint</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="nm">500</span>, <span class="nm">100</span>)
})

<span class="cm"># Dashboard metric 1: Revenue by region</span>
<span class="va">revenue_by_region</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">orders</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'region'</span>)[<span class="st">'amount'</span>].<span class="fn">sum</span>()

<span class="cm"># Dashboard metric 2: Orders, revenue, and avg order value by category</span>
<span class="va">category_metrics</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">orders</span>.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(<span class="st">'category'</span>).<span class="fn">agg</span>(
    <span class="va">order_count</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="st">'order_id'</span>, <span class="st">'count'</span>),
    <span class="va">total_revenue</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="st">'amount'</span>, <span class="st">'sum'</span>),
    <span class="va">avg_order_value</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="st">'amount'</span>, <span class="st">'mean'</span>)
)

<span class="cm"># Dashboard metric 3: Market share by category</span>
<span class="va">category_metrics</span>[<span class="st">'market_share'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> (
    <span class="va">category_metrics</span>[<span class="st">'total_revenue'</span>] <span class="op">/</span> <span class="va">category_metrics</span>[<span class="st">'total_revenue'</span>].<span class="fn">sum</span>()
)

<span class="cm"># Dashboard metric 4: Heatmap data - Region vs Category</span>
<span class="va">region_category</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">orders</span>.<span class="fn">pivot_table</span>(
    <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'region'</span>,
    <span class="va">columns</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'category'</span>,
    <span class="va">values</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'amount'</span>,
    <span class="va">aggfunc</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'sum'</span>
)</code></pre><div class="callout-box tip"><strong>Best Practice:</strong> Combine named aggregations with comments to create self-documenting code. Other analysts should understand metrics without additional explanation.</div><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>Calculate the average amount per region and sort by highest average.</li><li>Using transform(), add a column showing each order's percentage of its category total.</li><li>Create a pivot table with regions as rows, categories as columns, and count of orders as values.</li><li>Find which category has the highest revenue variance using groupby() and agg().</li><li>Use crosstab() to analyze the frequency of each region-category combination.</li></ol></section><section class="section" id="summary"><span class="section-label">Summary</span><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><strong>GroupBy Pattern:</strong> Split-apply-combine is the mental model. Group by key, apply function to each group, combine results.</li><li><strong>Simple Aggregations:</strong> Use .sum(), .mean(), .count() for quick statistics. Returns Series with group labels as index.</li><li><strong>Multiple Aggregations:</strong> Use .agg() with dictionaries for flexible column-to-function mapping. Named aggregations improve readability.</li><li><strong>Transform vs Agg:</strong> agg() reduces to one row per group; transform() returns full original shape. Use transform() for feature engineering.</li><li><strong>Pivot Tables:</strong> Reshape data with group keys becoming headers. Specify index, columns, values, and aggfunc.</li><li><strong>Cross-Tabulation:</strong> Analyze categorical relationships with crosstab(). Use margins=True for totals.</li><li><strong>Performance:</strong> groupby is optimized and fast. It's more efficient than manual looping for group-level operations.</li></ul></section><div class="nav-buttons"><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module3-lesson5" class="nav-btn prev"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M15 18l-6-6 6-6"/></svg> Previous</a><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module3-lesson7" class="nav-btn next">Next <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></div></div></main><footer><div class="container"><div class="footer-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" alt="SustainSys" class="footer-logo-img"><div class="footer-logo-text">Sustain<span>Sys</span> AI Academy</div></div><p>© 2026 SustainSys. All rights reserved.</p><p><a href="https://sustainsys.co.uk">sustainsys.co.uk</a></p></div></footer><script src="../assets/course-nav.js"><\/script></body></html>
